            <ArticleTitle>Improving screening for alcohol consumption during pregnancy with phosphatidylethanol.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>104-107</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0890-6238(17)30258-7</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.reprotox.2017.09.007</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">The study objective was to compare rates of alcohol use between urine ethanol testing and self- reporting (Method: 1) and Phosphatidylethanol (PEth) dried blood spot testing and self-reporting (Method: 2).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">This was a prospective observational study in an obstetric clinic with universal alcohol screening.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Method: 1 identified 11 patients with alcohol use (5 urine and 6 self-reported); Method: 2 identified 28 (22 PEth and 6 self-reported) out of 315 patients (one patient positive for both urine and PEth). The six patients with self-reported use had negative urine and PEth testing. We had fair agreement between the two methods (282 negative and 7 positive; 289/314=92.0%; Kappa 0.32, p&lt;0.001); method 2 identified significantly more women (McNemar, p&lt;0.001). Combining methods: resulted in an alcohol detection rate of 10.2% (32/314).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Method: 2 identified more alcohol users than Method: 1. Combining both methods: identified the most alcohol consumption.</AbstractText>
